WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.793
This short guide shows you how to read the DataHub App, page by page.

2
00:00:04.793 --> 00:00:07.089
For each screen, you'll learn three things.

3
00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:08.073
What it shows.

4
00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:09.385
How to read it.

5
00:00:09.385 --> 00:00:11.354
And what to look at next.

6
00:00:11.354 --> 00:00:13.650
All the figures are synthetic demo data.

7
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.604
Use the chapters to jump straight to the page you need.

8
00:00:17.604 --> 00:00:20.252
Some optional pages have no data in this demo.

9
00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:24.667
When that happens, we show the empty page, and explain what it would tell you.

10
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:27.953
Keep three kinds of number apart.

11
00:00:27.953 --> 00:00:32.264
Counted numbers are what really happened, like sessions or credits.

12
00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:37.869
Labelled numbers sort that activity using rules, like task types or fit grades.

13
00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:41.749
Estimated numbers add assumptions, like expert hours and value.

14
00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:44.767
Only counted numbers are a direct record.

15
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.688
Before you read any chart, check the data date at the top.

16
00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:52.512
It shows how far the loaded data goes.

17
00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:55.691
For example, audit log to the thirty-first of August.

18
00:00:55.691 --> 00:00:58.162
Different sources can end on different dates.

19
00:00:58.162 --> 00:01:00.988
A blank or a dash means no data.

20
00:01:00.988 --> 00:01:02.400
It doesn't mean zero.

21
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:07.945
Use the filter bar to choose dates, organisation, licence and activity type.

22
00:01:07.945 --> 00:01:13.577
Group by changes how people are split, for example by organisation or by country.

23
00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:15.991
Not every card follows every filter.

24
00:01:15.991 --> 00:01:20.417
If a card says not filtered by, it ignores that filter.

25
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:26.820
When you compare two numbers, keep the same dates, the same people and the same unit.

26
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.454
If one card covers three months and another covers one, they won't match.

27
00:01:31.454 --> 00:01:32.167
That's expected.

28
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:35.181
The bottom line answers two questions.

29
00:01:35.181 --> 00:01:38.652
The top row asks: is Copilot doing real work?

30
00:01:38.652 --> 00:01:42.510
The bottom row asks: is it becoming how people work?

31
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.367
Read the big number first, then the arrow beside it.

32
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:52.140
An up arrow means the last full month was higher than the month before.

33
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:57.977
Totals are compared per day, so a longer month doesn't look better just because it's longer.

34
00:01:57.977 --> 00:01:59.436
Points means percentage points.

35
00:01:59.436 --> 00:02:03.083
Going from seventy to seventy-two percent is up two points.

36
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:05.607
Expert hours are an estimate.

37
00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:07.066
Tasks are counted requests.

38
00:02:07.066 --> 00:02:11.444
The credits card adds Copilot Studio and Cowork for the whole tenant.

39
00:02:11.444 --> 00:02:16.186
Azure is billed in money, not credits, so it isn't in that total.

40
00:02:16.186 --> 00:02:19.833
If a number surprises you, open the page behind it.

41
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:24.518
This page shows two monthly charts on the same timeline.

42
00:02:24.518 --> 00:02:27.307
The top chart shows estimated expert hours.

43
00:02:27.307 --> 00:02:29.697
The bottom chart shows credits used.

44
00:02:29.697 --> 00:02:33.283
Each colour is the same source in both charts.

45
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.680
Read from left to right.

46
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.103
A taller bar means more that month.

47
00:02:38.103 --> 00:02:39.487
Then check the colours.

48
00:02:39.487 --> 00:02:45.025
If one colour grows and the others stay flat, the change comes from that one source.

49
00:02:45.025 --> 00:02:48.485
Here, hours peak in August, but credits peak in July.

50
00:02:48.485 --> 00:02:51.600
That's a reason to look closer, not a problem.

51
00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:55.468
Faded months sit outside your selected dates, for context.

52
00:02:55.468 --> 00:02:59.006
A last month that's only partly loaded will look low.

53
00:02:59.006 --> 00:03:02.899
So check the month is complete before you call a drop.

54
00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:06.083
Then open Value for hours, or Consumption for credits.

55
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:08.708
Each circle is an organisation.

56
00:03:08.708 --> 00:03:12.941
Further right means more of its licensed seats are in use.

57
00:03:12.941 --> 00:03:17.174
Higher up means more estimated expert hours per seat each month.

58
00:03:17.174 --> 00:03:19.483
A bigger circle means more seats.

59
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.892
The dashed lines mark the middle of the group.

60
00:03:22.892 --> 00:03:25.284
Top right is high use and high hours.

61
00:03:25.284 --> 00:03:27.078
Bottom left is lower on both.

62
00:03:27.078 --> 00:03:32.162
A big circle low down is a good place to offer help, because many seats are involved.

63
00:03:32.162 --> 00:03:36.050
A small circle high up may have a way of working worth sharing.

64
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:42.480
The table lists seats, seats in use, habit rate and hours per seat for each organisation.

65
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.453
Compare per-seat rates, so big teams don't win just by size.

66
00:03:46.453 --> 00:03:50.788
The bar chart shows which kinds of work add the most hours.

67
00:03:50.788 --> 00:03:54.400
People without a department are left out of these views.

68
00:03:55.117 --> 00:03:59.667
This page lists the few things the data says need attention, biggest impact first.

69
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:04.217
Each card says what it found, and links to the page with the detail.

70
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:06.803
On each card, read two things.

71
00:04:06.803 --> 00:04:11.319
The size of the gap, and how many ratings or people it rests on.

72
00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:16.482
A gap based on thirty ratings is weaker than the same gap based on three hundred.

73
00:04:16.482 --> 00:04:20.353
Extra hours on a card show what closing a gap could add.

74
00:04:20.353 --> 00:04:21.967
They're not hours already gained.

75
00:04:22.617 --> 00:04:25.462
Open Can we trust these numbers before you act.

76
00:04:25.462 --> 00:04:27.675
No card doesn't mean everything is fine.

77
00:04:27.675 --> 00:04:32.417
For each card you act on, name an owner and a date to check again.

78
00:04:33.117 --> 00:04:35.917
Adoption shows how often people come back.

79
00:04:35.917 --> 00:04:39.517
The main number is sessions per user per week.

80
00:04:39.517 --> 00:04:43.117
A session is one conversation thread in one app.

81
00:04:43.117 --> 00:04:47.517
There are cards for everyone, licensed chat, unlicensed chat and agents.

82
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.881
The weekly chart has one line per group.

83
00:04:50.881 --> 00:04:54.567
A line that stays high week after week shows a habit.

84
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:59.258
A single spike is usually an event, like a launch or a training day.

85
00:04:59.258 --> 00:05:04.284
If the licensed line sits above the unlicensed line, licensed users come back more often.

86
00:05:04.284 --> 00:05:07.300
That's a difference, not proof the licence caused it.

87
00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.585
Don't add up the user counts across cards.

88
00:05:10.585 --> 00:05:14.538
One person can use chat and agents, so they appear in both.

89
00:05:14.538 --> 00:05:19.150
Use the buttons at the top to switch between per user, sessions and hours.

90
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:21.992
Activation asks a simple question.

91
00:05:21.992 --> 00:05:24.967
Who has used Copilot at least once?

92
00:05:24.967 --> 00:05:29.217
Each card shows active people, inactive people and the total.

93
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:33.167
The percentage is active people divided by the total.

94
00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:39.767
For example, one hundred and eight active out of one hundred and twelve licensed is about ninety-six percent.

95
00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:41.600
High activation means wide reach.

96
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.533
It doesn't tell you if people come back.

97
00:05:44.533 --> 00:05:47.467
That's what Adoption and Habit formation are for.

98
00:05:48.117 --> 00:05:52.855
Open the organisation view to see which teams have the most inactive people.

99
00:05:52.855 --> 00:05:55.407
That's where first-use help will matter most.

100
00:05:55.407 --> 00:05:59.417
Check the licence list is correct before you call anyone inactive.

101
00:06:00.117 --> 00:06:07.000
Habit formation sorts licensed people by how many days they used Copilot in the last full month.

102
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.429
Beginner is one to five days.

103
00:06:09.429 --> 00:06:11.048
Developing, six to ten.

104
00:06:11.048 --> 00:06:12.668
Habitual, eleven to fifteen.

105
00:06:12.668 --> 00:06:14.287
Power, sixteen or more.

106
00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:16.717
Inactive means licensed, but no use.

107
00:06:17.367 --> 00:06:19.112
Switch between share and count.

108
00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.507
Share shows the mix.

109
00:06:20.507 --> 00:06:22.252
Count shows how many people.

110
00:06:22.252 --> 00:06:27.138
In the chart below, each bar is one month, with Power at the top.

111
00:06:27.138 --> 00:06:32.722
If the Power and Habitual bands grow over time, more people are using Copilot most days.

112
00:06:32.722 --> 00:06:34.467
That's the trend you want.

113
00:06:35.117 --> 00:06:38.667
If many people stay in Beginner, focus your support there.

114
00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:41.507
These bands measure how often, not how well.

115
00:06:41.507 --> 00:06:45.767
Someone who works part time may use it less, for good reasons.

116
00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:51.180
The heatmap shows one measure for every team and every week.

117
00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.470
Rows are organisations.

118
00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:53.760
Columns are weeks.

119
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.350
Pick the measure first: active users, active days, hours, or sessions per user.

120
00:07:00.033 --> 00:07:02.181
Darker cells mean more of that measure.

121
00:07:02.181 --> 00:07:05.249
Read across a row to follow one team over time.

122
00:07:05.249 --> 00:07:08.624
Read down a column to compare teams in the same week.

123
00:07:08.624 --> 00:07:13.533
A pale column across every team is often a holiday, or a gap in the data.

124
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.809
Look for patterns that last several weeks, not single cells.

125
00:07:17.809 --> 00:07:21.780
A team that fades over a month is worth a conversation.

126
00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.750
To compare big and small teams fairly, use a per-user measure.

127
00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:31.890
Task breakdown shows how much work Copilot was asked to do, and what kind.

128
00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:37.718
The top cards show tasks, and tasks per user, for everyone, licensed, unlicensed and agents.

129
00:07:37.718 --> 00:07:40.826
A task is one request on one item.

130
00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:45.100
So one prompt that reads three files counts as three tasks.

131
00:07:45.783 --> 00:07:47.862
The bar chart ranks task types.

132
00:07:47.862 --> 00:07:50.979
A longer bar means more tasks of that kind.

133
00:07:50.979 --> 00:07:56.521
If one bar is twice as long as another, that type had twice as many tasks.

134
00:07:56.521 --> 00:08:00.677
Use break down by to switch to workflow action or value outcome.

135
00:08:00.677 --> 00:08:04.833
Task types are labelled by rules, so treat them as a guide.

136
00:08:05.533 --> 00:08:11.864
Lower down, two charts show where requests came from, like Teams or Outlook, and which model answered.

137
00:08:11.864 --> 00:08:16.704
No model logged means the model wasn't recorded, not that none was used.

138
00:08:16.704 --> 00:08:19.683
Use this to spot common uses worth sharing.

139
00:08:20.367 --> 00:08:26.078
Estimated value asks: how long would this work take an expert, and what is that worth?

140
00:08:26.078 --> 00:08:28.219
It's a model, not measured savings.

141
00:08:28.219 --> 00:08:33.217
The top cards show hours per week, value per week and a yearly figure.

142
00:08:33.867 --> 00:08:39.147
Choose Conservative, Typical or Optimistic to set how long tasks are assumed to take.

143
00:08:39.147 --> 00:08:41.032
Then set your hourly rate.

144
00:08:41.032 --> 00:08:44.804
Changing the rate changes the money, but not the hours.

145
00:08:44.804 --> 00:08:47.067
Changing the effort level changes both.

146
00:08:47.783 --> 00:08:49.221
Here's a simple example.

147
00:08:49.221 --> 00:08:53.533
Twenty estimated hours at fifty dollars an hour is one thousand dollars.

148
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:57.486
Double the rate, and the value doubles to two thousand dollars.

149
00:08:57.486 --> 00:08:59.283
The hours stay at twenty.

150
00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:03.662
The value table shows which kinds of work add the most.

151
00:09:03.662 --> 00:09:07.038
Open a row to see the task types inside it.

152
00:09:07.038 --> 00:09:10.075
The agents table ranks named agents the same way.

153
00:09:10.075 --> 00:09:13.788
The yearly figure just scales your dates up to a year.

154
00:09:13.788 --> 00:09:15.138
It isn't a forecast.

155
00:09:15.138 --> 00:09:18.850
Quote the range from Conservative to Optimistic, not one big number.

156
00:09:19.533 --> 00:09:25.320
This page shows estimated expert hours per person in the last full month, for each habit band.

157
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.383
Each bar is per person, not a team total.

158
00:09:29.033 --> 00:09:32.924
A taller bar means more hours for each person in that band.

159
00:09:32.924 --> 00:09:39.409
If Power users show about seventeen hours and Beginners about two, a Power user logs around eight times as much.

160
00:09:39.409 --> 00:09:42.003
The curve below shows how concentrated hours are.

161
00:09:42.003 --> 00:09:47.191
The further it bows from the straight line, the more a few people carry the total.

162
00:09:47.191 --> 00:09:50.433
A dash means the group was too small to show.

163
00:09:51.117 --> 00:09:55.522
Frequent users show more hours, but that doesn't prove habit creates value.

164
00:09:55.522 --> 00:09:59.194
Heavy users may just have more work that suits Copilot.

165
00:09:59.194 --> 00:10:03.967
Use this to find ways of working worth sharing, not to rank people.

166
00:10:04.617 --> 00:10:10.513
Cost versus value sets estimated value against what Copilot cost over the same dates.

167
00:10:10.513 --> 00:10:15.567
The main number, return on cost, is estimated value divided by cost.

168
00:10:16.283 --> 00:10:20.120
Above one times, the estimated value is bigger than the cost.

169
00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.561
Below one times, the cost is bigger.

170
00:10:22.561 --> 00:10:27.443
The break-even rate is the lowest hourly rate at which value still covers cost.

171
00:10:27.443 --> 00:10:30.233
The lower it is, the stronger the case.

172
00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:38.753
For example, one thousand dollars of estimated value, divided by four hundred dollars of cost, is two and a half times.

173
00:10:38.753 --> 00:10:41.726
Remember, the value is modelled, not cash saved.

174
00:10:41.726 --> 00:10:45.814
And the cost is licences plus credits at your set rates.

175
00:10:45.814 --> 00:10:47.300
It's not an invoice.

176
00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.624
The bars and table pair each cost with the work it pays for.

177
00:10:52.624 --> 00:10:54.422
Licences with licensed users' work.

178
00:10:54.422 --> 00:10:55.861
Studio credits with agents.

179
00:10:55.861 --> 00:10:57.299
Cowork credits with Cowork.

180
00:10:57.299 --> 00:11:00.535
The agent chart does the same for each agent.

181
00:11:00.535 --> 00:11:04.850
Check the Conservative figure before you use the number in a decision.

182
00:11:05.533 --> 00:11:12.710
All products puts Cowork and Work IQ, Copilot Studio and Azure side by side, over the same dates.

183
00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:20.683
The bar at the top shows the rates used: the pay-as-you-go rate, the prepaid rate, and your Capacity Pack balance.

184
00:11:21.367 --> 00:11:23.592
Credits are an amount of use.

185
00:11:23.592 --> 00:11:27.300
Cost is those credits priced at the rates you set.

186
00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.896
It's a calculated figure, not an invoice.

187
00:11:29.896 --> 00:11:31.008
Azure is different.

188
00:11:31.008 --> 00:11:34.717
It's already money, from billing, so it has no credits.

189
00:11:35.367 --> 00:11:37.414
Read the table side by side.

190
00:11:37.414 --> 00:11:39.120
Never add credits to dollars.

191
00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:43.214
The bar chart shows which product costs most over the same dates.

192
00:11:43.214 --> 00:11:46.967
If one product jumps, open its own tab to see why.

193
00:11:47.617 --> 00:11:54.617
Budget runway shows each product's spend so far in its latest loaded month, against an optional monthly budget.

194
00:11:55.283 --> 00:12:01.583
Each card shows the spend so far, and where the month ends if the same daily pace continues.

195
00:12:01.583 --> 00:12:05.083
The status can be On track, At risk or Over.

196
00:12:05.083 --> 00:12:07.883
No budget just means none has been set.

197
00:12:07.883 --> 00:12:09.283
It's not a warning.

198
00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:14.246
For example, one hundred dollars in ten days is ten dollars a day.

199
00:12:14.246 --> 00:12:16.889
Over thirty days, that's about three hundred dollars.

200
00:12:16.889 --> 00:12:21.185
With a budget of two hundred and fifty, the card warns you early.

201
00:12:21.185 --> 00:12:25.150
It's a straight line from the pace so far, not a forecast.

202
00:12:25.867 --> 00:12:30.803
This page shows weekly Cowork and Work IQ credits, by person or group.

203
00:12:30.803 --> 00:12:38.017
The cards show credits used, people using credits, credits per user, and how much of their allowance they've used.

204
00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.905
Switch to Cost to see money.

205
00:12:40.905 --> 00:12:43.844
Cost uses the Capacity Pack balance you enter.

206
00:12:43.844 --> 00:12:48.253
It's applied once to the whole selected period, not once a month.

207
00:12:48.253 --> 00:12:51.928
Credits use up that balance first, at the prepaid rate.

208
00:12:51.928 --> 00:12:54.500
Anything beyond it is priced at pay-as-you-go.

209
00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:05.298
For example, with a six thousand credit balance and ten thousand credits used, six thousand are priced at the prepaid rate, and four thousand at pay-as-you-go.

210
00:13:05.298 --> 00:13:08.016
The weekly chart shows when use peaks.

211
00:13:08.016 --> 00:13:11.900
Allowance used only means something where a limit is set.

212
00:13:12.617 --> 00:13:16.089
Copilot Studio shows the credits your agents use.

213
00:13:16.089 --> 00:13:22.167
The cards show total credits, active users, agents with credits, and credits per user.

214
00:13:22.867 --> 00:13:27.727
The main chart shows credits each day, stacked as prepaid and pay-as-you-go.

215
00:13:27.727 --> 00:13:30.562
That split comes from Microsoft's own data.

216
00:13:30.562 --> 00:13:35.422
The line is a seven-day average, so it smooths out busy days.

217
00:13:35.422 --> 00:13:39.067
A rising line means agents are using more credits.

218
00:13:39.783 --> 00:13:43.013
Next, see which models and features use the credits.

219
00:13:43.013 --> 00:13:47.319
Not recorded means the source didn't say, not that nothing was used.

220
00:13:47.319 --> 00:13:50.548
The agent and user tables show each one's share.

221
00:13:50.548 --> 00:13:55.213
If one agent takes a large share, check what it does before acting.

222
00:13:55.213 --> 00:13:58.083
High use isn't the same as high value.

223
00:13:58.783 --> 00:14:04.256
The Azure tab shows what the Azure side of the solution costs, in money.

224
00:14:04.256 --> 00:14:09.338
It isn't measured in credits, so it's never part of the credits total.

225
00:14:09.338 --> 00:14:11.292
First, choose Actual or Amortized.

226
00:14:11.292 --> 00:14:13.638
Actual shows charges when they happen.

227
00:14:13.638 --> 00:14:15.983
Amortized spreads up-front purchases over time.

228
00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:21.470
Model share is the part of Azure cost spent on AI models.

229
00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:25.445
Supporting cost is everything around them, like storage and search.

230
00:14:25.445 --> 00:14:29.022
The other cards count usage, like requests and tokens.

231
00:14:29.022 --> 00:14:32.600
Cost per million tokens is a simple unit rate.

232
00:14:33.283 --> 00:14:36.929
The daily chart shows how cost moves over time.

233
00:14:36.929 --> 00:14:40.576
The service bars show which Azure services cost most.

234
00:14:40.576 --> 00:14:46.247
The resource table shows each resource, and the department its tag assigns it to.

235
00:14:46.247 --> 00:14:49.083
Untagged cost isn't shared out across teams.

236
00:14:49.783 --> 00:14:53.410
Cowork fit asks whether the work people give Cowork suits it.

237
00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:57.696
Each session gets a grade, based on the apps and sources it used.

238
00:14:57.696 --> 00:15:00.333
It doesn't judge the quality of the answer.

239
00:15:01.033 --> 00:15:06.885
Strong fit means work across two or more apps, building a file, or using many sources.

240
00:15:06.885 --> 00:15:10.543
Fair fit means some sources, or work inside one app.

241
00:15:10.543 --> 00:15:13.834
Worth a look means plain chat with nothing attached.

242
00:15:13.834 --> 00:15:16.760
Everyday Copilot Chat could probably have done it.

243
00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:20.783
The bar chart shows the mix for each type of task.

244
00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:23.334
Shares only count graded sessions.

245
00:15:23.334 --> 00:15:30.871
For example, forty Strong out of eighty graded is fifty percent, even if twenty more were too light to grade.

246
00:15:30.871 --> 00:15:36.900
A high Worth a look share is a chance to coach people, not proof of waste.

247
00:15:37.617 --> 00:15:42.943
Model fit compares how heavy the work is with the kind of model used.

248
00:15:42.943 --> 00:15:44.464
Frontier models are premium.

249
00:15:44.464 --> 00:15:46.367
Workhorse models are mid cost.

250
00:15:47.033 --> 00:15:50.062
Good match means the model suits the work.

251
00:15:50.062 --> 00:15:54.226
Lighter model may do means a premium model did light work.

252
00:15:54.226 --> 00:15:57.633
Try stronger means a mid-cost model did heavy work.

253
00:15:57.633 --> 00:16:00.283
Not judged means there wasn't enough information.

254
00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:03.887
The tool chart compares agents, Copilot and Cowork.

255
00:16:03.887 --> 00:16:09.760
The table shows the verdict for each task type, and needs at least five judged sessions.

256
00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:12.696
Use a flag to pick something to test.

257
00:16:12.696 --> 00:16:16.000
It isn't proof of savings, or of poor answers.

258
00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:20.983
The leaderboard ranks people, teams, apps and tasks by sessions.

259
00:16:20.983 --> 00:16:26.550
The top cards show active users, sessions, and sessions per user per week.

260
00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:31.224
The concentration cards show how much use comes from the busiest people.

261
00:16:31.224 --> 00:16:37.259
For example, if the top five percent of people make thirteen percent of sessions, use is fairly spread.

262
00:16:37.259 --> 00:16:40.947
If they made half, a few people would be carrying it.

263
00:16:40.947 --> 00:16:44.300
A dash means the group was too small to show.

264
00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:48.850
Use this to find people who could share good ways of working.

265
00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:52.750
Lots of sessions can also mean a heavy role, or repeated tries.

266
00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.700
It's not a performance league table.

267
00:16:55.367 --> 00:16:59.083
The Agents tab shows which agents people actually use.

268
00:16:59.083 --> 00:17:06.517
The cards show agent users, their share of all active users, agent sessions, and sessions per agent user.

269
00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.774
The bar chart ranks agents by sessions.

270
00:17:09.774 --> 00:17:11.981
A longer bar means more use.

271
00:17:11.981 --> 00:17:20.808
The three highlight cards show the most used agent, the one used by the most job functions, and the one reaching the most organisations.

272
00:17:20.808 --> 00:17:23.750
Wide reach means many teams find it useful.

273
00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:30.030
The table lists each agent with its owner, who can use it, and when it was last used.

274
00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:33.750
An agent with no matched use may just have a naming gap.

275
00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.850
Sessions show use, not whether the agent solved the problem.

276
00:17:37.533 --> 00:17:41.230
Work patterns needs an optional Microsoft 365 data source.

277
00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:45.337
This demo doesn't include it, so the page is empty.

278
00:17:45.337 --> 00:17:46.980
Empty means not connected.

279
00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:49.033
It doesn't mean zero activity.

280
00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:55.748
When connected, this tab shows how people work across Teams, Outlook, SharePoint and OneDrive.

281
00:17:55.748 --> 00:18:00.500
Read active people separately from counts of meetings, emails and calls.

282
00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:05.000
Use it to see how Copilot fits into everyday work.

283
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.800
Don't judge anyone's productivity by their email or meeting counts.

284
00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:14.300
Apps and devices uses the same optional source, so it's empty here too.

285
00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:20.715
When connected, it shows which Microsoft 365 apps people use, how many, and on which devices.

286
00:18:20.715 --> 00:18:27.200
One person can use several devices, so the shares can add up to more than one hundred percent.

287
00:18:27.867 --> 00:18:31.917
Use it to tailor training to the apps and devices people actually use.

288
00:18:32.617 --> 00:18:37.190
This tab would compare Copilot users with other Microsoft 365 users.

289
00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:40.517
It's empty here, because the source isn't connected.

290
00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:47.523
When connected, a ratio above one means Copilot users do more of that activity per person.

291
00:18:47.523 --> 00:18:51.079
For example, one point two means twenty percent more.

292
00:18:51.079 --> 00:18:53.450
It doesn't prove Copilot caused it.

293
00:18:54.117 --> 00:18:59.917
Use it to find licensed people who aren't using Copilot yet, and to choose a way of working to try.

294
00:19:00.617 --> 00:19:05.607
License readiness shows people using Copilot without a licence, and licences sitting idle.

295
00:19:05.607 --> 00:19:12.517
The cards show active unlicensed users, their share of all active users, and how often they use it.

296
00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:17.119
The bar chart shows which organisations have the busiest unlicensed users.

297
00:19:17.119 --> 00:19:19.969
The table ranks people by a priority score.

298
00:19:19.969 --> 00:19:26.737
It's based on how many tasks they do, and how many days they're active each week they use Copilot.

299
00:19:26.737 --> 00:19:28.162
Higher means stronger demand.

300
00:19:28.162 --> 00:19:30.300
It isn't a chance of success.

301
00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:34.896
Use the top of the list for a conversation or a short trial.

302
00:19:34.896 --> 00:19:40.057
Idle seats are licences with no use for thirty days or more, so they could be moved.

303
00:19:40.057 --> 00:19:43.700
Check the licence list is up to date before you reassign anyone.

304
00:19:44.367 --> 00:19:46.710
This tab looks at the last full month.

305
00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:50.517
It splits people by habit band, and by whether they have a licence.

306
00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:53.206
Read the share unlicensed column.

307
00:19:53.206 --> 00:19:57.218
It's the share of each habit band with no licence.

308
00:19:57.218 --> 00:20:02.835
For example, if two of twenty-seven Habitual users have no licence, that's seven percent.

309
00:20:02.835 --> 00:20:07.650
Unlicensed people in the Habitual or Power bands show the strongest demand.

310
00:20:08.367 --> 00:20:11.732
The list below shows the most active unlicensed people.

311
00:20:11.732 --> 00:20:16.967
Ask about their work first, then try a time-limited licence and check the results.

312
00:20:17.617 --> 00:20:24.667
Cowork readiness lists Copilot users who haven't tried Cowork yet, with the broadest and deepest users first.

313
00:20:25.367 --> 00:20:27.293
Three things raise someone's rank.

314
00:20:27.293 --> 00:20:30.375
Breadth: how many apps they use each day.

315
00:20:30.375 --> 00:20:32.687
Depth: how many prompts per session.

316
00:20:32.687 --> 00:20:34.999
And whether they already use agents.

317
00:20:34.999 --> 00:20:38.467
Higher on all three means higher on the list.

318
00:20:39.117 --> 00:20:41.829
Rank compares people with each other in your selection.

319
00:20:41.829 --> 00:20:44.540
Change the dates or teams, and ranks can move.

320
00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:50.567
Offer Cowork to the people at the top as a trial, and check they have access and a real need.

321
00:20:51.283 --> 00:20:54.563
Agent Evaluation needs Copilot Studio conversation data.

322
00:20:54.563 --> 00:20:59.717
This demo has none, so it says No agent conversations found.

323
00:20:59.717 --> 00:21:01.591
That means no data.

324
00:21:01.591 --> 00:21:03.933
It doesn't mean zero failures.

325
00:21:04.617 --> 00:21:12.170
When connected, Performance shows conversations, how many the agent resolved, failures, hand-offs to people, and satisfaction.

326
00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:15.474
Higher resolved and lower failure is better.

327
00:21:15.474 --> 00:21:20.667
Satisfaction is thumbs up, divided by thumbs up plus thumbs down.

328
00:21:21.367 --> 00:21:23.220
Watch the trends over time.

329
00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.667
If failures rise, review some of those conversations to find out why.

330
00:21:28.367 --> 00:21:31.767
Conversations and topics shares the same empty state.

331
00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:35.870
When connected, it shows each topic's volume and friction.

332
00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:40.050
Friction runs from zero to one hundred, and higher is worse.

333
00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:43.090
Grounded means the agent used an internal source.

334
00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:45.750
That doesn't prove the answer was right.

335
00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:50.494
Start with topics that are both busy and high in friction.

336
00:21:50.494 --> 00:21:52.700
Fixing those helps the most people.

337
00:21:53.367 --> 00:21:55.709
Governance helps you review your agents.

338
00:21:55.709 --> 00:21:58.831
It's a review aid, not a compliance certificate.

339
00:21:58.831 --> 00:22:05.467
Estate health shows how many agents are registered, their lifecycle stage, and how many have review flags.

340
00:22:06.117 --> 00:22:08.005
Read the coverage note first.

341
00:22:08.005 --> 00:22:10.270
It says which sources are loaded.

342
00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:12.913
A check that couldn't run is unavailable.

343
00:22:12.913 --> 00:22:14.046
It hasn't passed.

344
00:22:14.046 --> 00:22:17.067
The lifecycle bars show where agents sit today.

345
00:22:17.783 --> 00:22:21.311
Use the flags to decide which agents to check first.

346
00:22:21.311 --> 00:22:24.133
Confirm the current setup before you change anything.

347
00:22:24.783 --> 00:22:27.233
Exposure places each agent on two axes.

348
00:22:27.233 --> 00:22:29.333
One is who it's shared with.

349
00:22:29.333 --> 00:22:32.133
The other is what data it can read.

350
00:22:32.783 --> 00:22:40.182
An agent shared with the whole organisation, that can read organisational content, sits in the corner worth reviewing first.

351
00:22:40.182 --> 00:22:42.518
That doesn't mean data has leaked.

352
00:22:42.518 --> 00:22:44.076
Not reported means unknown.

353
00:22:44.076 --> 00:22:45.633
It doesn't mean safe.

354
00:22:46.283 --> 00:22:49.517
Ask the owner if the audience and access are intended.

355
00:22:49.517 --> 00:22:51.133
Broad sharing can be fine.

356
00:22:51.783 --> 00:22:55.283
Accountability shows whether each agent has a known owner.

357
00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.655
The chart splits agents by owner status.

358
00:22:58.655 --> 00:23:02.132
Active, disabled, not found, no owner, or not checked.

359
00:23:02.132 --> 00:23:03.291
Active is good.

360
00:23:03.291 --> 00:23:07.155
Disabled or not found may mean the owner has left.

361
00:23:07.155 --> 00:23:08.700
Not checked means unknown.

362
00:23:09.367 --> 00:23:15.667
For any agent without an active owner, find a responsible person or team before you retire or reassign it.

363
00:23:16.367 --> 00:23:20.367
The review queue lists agents with flags, most important first.

364
00:23:21.033 --> 00:23:23.033
Each row lists its reasons.

365
00:23:23.033 --> 00:23:28.233
For example, a missing owner, shared with everyone, or shared but never used.

366
00:23:28.233 --> 00:23:30.633
More flags put an agent higher.

367
00:23:30.633 --> 00:23:32.233
Higher means review sooner.

368
00:23:32.233 --> 00:23:34.633
It doesn't mean harm has happened.

369
00:23:35.283 --> 00:23:41.492
For each agent, confirm the setup, find the owner, and decide: keep, fix or retire.

370
00:23:41.492 --> 00:23:42.733
Write down why.

371
00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:48.933
Shadow AI uses Microsoft Defender data about AI tools seen on devices and networks.

372
00:23:48.933 --> 00:23:53.633
This demo has none, so it says Defender is not turned on.

373
00:23:53.633 --> 00:23:55.200
That means no data.

374
00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.550
It doesn't mean no shadow AI.

375
00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:03.829
When connected, it shows AI tools run, AI sites visited, and AI software installed.

376
00:24:03.829 --> 00:24:07.850
Devices and people are different counts, so don't mix them.

377
00:24:08.533 --> 00:24:12.226
Treat it as the minimum you can see, not a full count.

378
00:24:12.226 --> 00:24:16.533
Work with your security team to check use and policy, not to blame people.

379
00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:22.705
Feedback shows the thumbs up and thumbs down people submit in Microsoft 365.

380
00:24:22.705 --> 00:24:27.362
The cards show total items, thumbs up, thumbs down and satisfaction.

381
00:24:27.362 --> 00:24:30.750
Satisfaction is thumbs up divided by all feedback.

382
00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:36.464
In the weekly chart, thumbs up sit above zero, and thumbs down below.

383
00:24:36.464 --> 00:24:40.321
A deeper bar below means more negative feedback that week.

384
00:24:40.321 --> 00:24:42.250
Compare it with the total.

385
00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:44.950
More complaints can simply mean more use.

386
00:24:45.617 --> 00:24:48.860
The theme and category charts show what people talk about.

387
00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:53.077
Look for a theme with a low thumbs-up share and lots of ratings.

388
00:24:53.077 --> 00:24:54.374
That's worth following up.

389
00:24:54.374 --> 00:24:58.267
Themes are sorted by keywords, so read a few comments to check.

390
00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:03.690
Time per task shows the minutes assumed for each kind of task.

391
00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:06.850
These numbers turn tasks into estimated expert hours.

392
00:25:07.533 --> 00:25:13.679
If you raise the minutes for a task, its estimated hours and value go up.

393
00:25:13.679 --> 00:25:17.776
Conservative, Typical and Optimistic give low, middle and high values.

394
00:25:17.776 --> 00:25:19.825
Use research restores the defaults.

395
00:25:19.825 --> 00:25:22.283
Cowork has its own time bands.

396
00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:26.783
In a real deployment, these settings are shared by everyone.

397
00:25:26.783 --> 00:25:29.850
Agree any change with others, and note why.

398
00:25:30.533 --> 00:25:32.475
The glossary defines each term.

399
00:25:32.475 --> 00:25:37.133
Search for the one you're reading, like task, session or active day.

400
00:25:37.783 --> 00:25:40.040
For any number, ask four questions.

401
00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.168
What is counted?

402
00:25:41.168 --> 00:25:42.296
Who is included?

403
00:25:42.296 --> 00:25:44.177
What is it divided by?

404
00:25:44.177 --> 00:25:46.433
And what dates does it cover?

405
00:25:47.117 --> 00:25:52.767
When you share a screenshot, include the definition and the filters, so others read it the same way.

406
00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:57.850
Signal to Impact shows how the app turns activity into value.

407
00:25:57.850 --> 00:25:59.450
First, a recorded event.

408
00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:02.650
Then a rule labels the type of task.

409
00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:05.850
Then assumed minutes turn it into estimated hours.

410
00:26:06.533 --> 00:26:08.553
Each step adds an assumption.

411
00:26:08.553 --> 00:26:12.187
A recorded event isn't a finished piece of work.

412
00:26:12.187 --> 00:26:14.610
A label isn't a human review.

413
00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:17.033
Estimated hours aren't measured time saved.

414
00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:22.350
When someone asks where the value number comes from, walk them through these three steps.

415
00:26:23.033 --> 00:26:26.240
How grading works explains the fit and model rules.

416
00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:28.733
Use it when a grade needs explaining.

417
00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.762
Check which setting is used.

418
00:26:31.762 --> 00:26:33.612
Balanced, Strict or Lenient.

419
00:26:33.612 --> 00:26:35.000
Strict grades harder.

420
00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:36.850
Lenient grades more gently.

421
00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:40.550
Missing data leaves work ungraded, not graded badly.

422
00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:45.337
If a grade looks wrong, check the apps and sources behind it.

423
00:26:45.337 --> 00:26:47.750
Test any change on real work first.

424
00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:51.600
Ask lets you type a question in plain words.

425
00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:55.100
In this demo, it's a local example using synthetic data.

426
00:26:55.783 --> 00:27:01.541
In a live deployment, Ask answers using the app's own queries, and shows which ones it used.

427
00:27:01.541 --> 00:27:06.283
Page filters don't apply, so say the dates, team and measure in your question.

428
00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:10.050
Before you share an answer, check it on the matching page.

429
00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.809
Reports gather existing pages into a story you can share.

430
00:27:14.809 --> 00:27:20.150
Exec pre-read starts with the headline picture, then links cost with estimated value.

431
00:27:20.867 --> 00:27:24.179
Read each section just as you would its original page.

432
00:27:24.179 --> 00:27:25.504
Estimates are still estimates.

433
00:27:25.504 --> 00:27:28.485
Check the filters and data dates before you export.

434
00:27:28.485 --> 00:27:31.467
Use Save a copy to make your own version.

435
00:27:32.117 --> 00:27:36.644
Finance weekly groups the spending and value pages for a finance audience.

436
00:27:36.644 --> 00:27:40.417
Start with the cost basis, the currency and the dates.

437
00:27:41.117 --> 00:27:42.798
Keep three things apart.

438
00:27:42.798 --> 00:27:44.059
Billed Azure cost.

439
00:27:44.059 --> 00:27:45.320
Calculated credit cost.

440
00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.581
And estimated value.

441
00:27:46.581 --> 00:27:52.467
A month-end projection, or a yearly value, is not a forecast or an invoice.

442
00:27:53.117 --> 00:27:57.867
Adoption review groups reach, habit and readiness for an enablement conversation.

443
00:27:58.533 --> 00:28:04.407
Read activation as first use, adoption as coming back, and habit as days used last month.

444
00:28:04.407 --> 00:28:08.078
Pages with no data are skipped, not shown as zero.

445
00:28:08.078 --> 00:28:12.483
Use the pack to agree a small trial, then check back later.

446
00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:18.059
New pack lets you choose pages, put them in order, and name your own report.

447
00:28:18.059 --> 00:28:20.650
In the demo, it stays in your browser.

448
00:28:21.367 --> 00:28:25.242
PDF and PowerPoint exports include the filters and data dates.

449
00:28:25.242 --> 00:28:28.342
Once exported, files leave the app's access controls.

450
00:28:28.342 --> 00:28:32.217
So check for names and small groups before you share.

451
00:28:32.867 --> 00:28:34.883
Here's a simple reading order.

452
00:28:34.883 --> 00:28:36.899
Check the data dates first.

453
00:28:36.899 --> 00:28:40.527
Use Executive summary and Adoption to find the question.

454
00:28:40.527 --> 00:28:44.156
Use Value and Consumption to see effort and cost.

455
00:28:44.156 --> 00:28:46.978
Use Efficiency and Readiness to pick trials.

456
00:28:46.978 --> 00:28:51.817
Then use Feedback, Agent Evaluation and Governance to check quality and ownership.

457
00:28:52.533 --> 00:28:56.844
For every number, ask: is it counted, labelled, or estimated?

458
00:28:56.844 --> 00:28:59.430
More activity isn't automatically more value.

459
00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:05.033
Use the DataHub App to ask better questions, and then test the answers.
